# Lab 08 – Configure Swap Partitions and LVM Swap

## Objective

Create and configure both a traditional swap partition and an LVM-based swap Logical Volume. Configure both swap areas for persistent activation through `/etc/fstab`, activate them, and verify their status.

This lab also reinforces the relationship between Physical Volumes (PVs), Volume Groups (VGs), Logical Volumes (LVs), and the storage pool provided by LVM.

---

## Background

Linux swap provides disk-backed space that can be used when physical memory is under pressure.

Swap can be implemented in multiple ways. This lab demonstrated two methods:

1. A traditional swap partition.
2. An LVM Logical Volume configured as swap.

The two configurations were:

```text
Traditional Swap:

/dev/sde1
   ↓
swap
   ↓
/etc/fstab
   ↓
swapon -a
```

and:

```text
LVM Swap:

/dev/sde2
   ↓
PV
   ↓
vg200
   ↓
swapVol
   ↓
swap
   ↓
/etc/fstab
   ↓
swapon -a
```

