## Procedure

### Step 1 – Identify an Available Disk

Used:

```bash
lsblk
```

to identify an available approximately 400 MB disk.

The disk selected for the lab was:

```text
/dev/sde
```

The disk had existing storage information, so it was cleaned and prepared for the lab.

---

## Step 2 – Create an MBR Partition Table

The disk was initialized with an MBR partition table.

The MBR partition table is represented by the `msdos` label in `parted`.

The resulting partitioning scheme used the disk as an MBR-partitioned device.

The important distinction is:

```text
MBR/GPT
   ↓
Partition table format
```

whereas:

```text
swap/ext4/XFS
   ↓
Filesystem/storage structure inside a partition or LV
```

---

## Step 3 – Create Two Swap/LVM Partitions

The lab required two approximately 100 MB partitions.

The first partition was designated for traditional swap:

```text
/dev/sde1
```

The second partition was designated for LVM:

```text
/dev/sde2
```

The resulting storage layout was conceptually:

```text
/dev/sde
├── /dev/sde1 → swap
└── /dev/sde2 → LVM PV
```

---

## Step 4 – Initialize Partition 1 as Swap

Initialized `/dev/sde1` as swap:

```bash
sudo mkswap /dev/sde1
```

This created a swap signature on the partition.

The storage relationship became:

```text
/dev/sde1
   ↓
swap
```

---

## Step 5 – Apply the Swap Label

Applied the required label:

```bash
sudo mkswap -L swappart /dev/sde1
```

The swap partition was now identified by the label:

```text
swappart
```

The label could be verified using:

```bash
lsblk -f
```

---

## Step 6 – Configure the Swap Partition in /etc/fstab

The swap partition was added to `/etc/fstab` using its appropriate swap identification.

The purpose of the `/etc/fstab` entry was to ensure that the swap area would be activated automatically when the system started.

The general configuration was:

```text
swap partition → /etc/fstab → automatic activation
```

---

## Step 7 – Activate the Swap Partition

Executed:

```bash
sudo swapon -a
```

The `-a` option activates all applicable swap entries configured in `/etc/fstab`.

The swap configuration was then verified using:

```bash
sudo swapon -s
```

Confirmed that the traditional swap partition was active.

