# LVM Swap Configuration

## Step 8 – Initialize Partition 2 as an LVM Physical Volume

Initialized `/dev/sde2` as an LVM Physical Volume:

```bash
sudo pvcreate /dev/sde2
```

Verified the PV:

```bash
sudo pvs
```

Confirmed that `/dev/sde2` was recognized as a Physical Volume.

The storage hierarchy was now:

```text
/dev/sde2
   ↓
PV
```

---

## Step 9 – Add the New PV to vg200

Added `/dev/sde2` to the existing Volume Group:

```bash
sudo vgextend vg200 /dev/sde2
```

Verified the Volume Group:

```bash
sudo vgs
```

The existing `vg200` Volume Group now contained multiple Physical Volumes.

Conceptually:

```text
/dev/sdc ───┐
            │
            ├── vg200
            │
/dev/sde2 ──┘
```

This increased the total storage available within the Volume Group.

---

## Step 10 – Verify the LVM Configuration

The PV configuration was verified with:

```bash
sudo pvs
```

The Volume Group was verified with:

```bash
sudo vgs
```

Logical Volumes were verified with:

```bash
sudo lvs
```

Detailed Volume Group information was verified with:

```bash
sudo vgdisplay vg200
```

These commands confirmed that `/dev/sde2` had successfully been added to `vg200`.

---

## Step 11 – Create the swapVol Logical Volume

The lab required a 180 MB Logical Volume named `swapVol`.

Created the Logical Volume:

```bash
sudo lvcreate -L 180M -n swapVol vg200
```

The Logical Volume was created from free space in `vg200`.

---

## Important LVM Concept – LV Allocation Across PVs

During the lab, it initially appeared that `swapVol` had been allocated from `/dev/sdc` rather than specifically from `/dev/sde2`.

This was investigated using:

```bash
sudo lvs -o lv_name,lv_size,vg_name,devices
```

This behavior was correct.

An important LVM principle was demonstrated:

> A Logical Volume belongs to the Volume Group, not directly to a particular Physical Volume.

Because both `/dev/sdc` and `/dev/sde2` were members of `vg200`, LVM was able to allocate the 180 MB Logical Volume from available Physical Extents in the Volume Group.

The conceptual structure was:

```text
/dev/sdc ───┐
            │
            ├── vg200 ──→ swapVol
            │
/dev/sde2 ──┘
```

LVM does not automatically interpret:

```text
vgextend vg200 /dev/sde2
```

as:

> "The next Logical Volume must be created on `/dev/sde2`."

Instead, the Volume Group acts as a pool of available Physical Extents.

---

## Step 12 – Attempt to Force swapVol onto /dev/sde2

To better understand LVM allocation, an attempt was made to explicitly allocate the 180 MB LV from `/dev/sde2`.

The attempt failed because `/dev/sde2` was only approximately 100 MB in size.

Therefore:

```text
/dev/sde2
≈ 100 MB available
```

while:

```text
swapVol
= 180 MB
```

The entire LV could not fit on that single PV.

This reinforced another important LVM concept:

> An LV can span multiple Physical Volumes when the Volume Group has sufficient free extents.

Therefore, the correct solution was to create `swapVol` from the Volume Group without restricting it to `/dev/sde2`.

---

## Step 13 – Initialize swapVol as Swap

Initialized the Logical Volume as swap:

```bash
sudo mkswap /dev/vg200/swapVol
```

The resulting storage structure became:

```text
vg200
   ↓
swapVol
   ↓
swap
```

---

## Step 14 – Add swapVol to /etc/fstab

The new LVM-based swap area was added to `/etc/fstab` using its device file.

The configuration referenced:

```text
/dev/vg200/swapVol
```

This ensures that the LVM swap area can be activated automatically during system startup.

---

## Step 15 – Activate the LVM Swap

Executed:

```bash
sudo swapon -a
```

This activated the swap entry configured in `/etc/fstab`.

---

## Step 16 – Verify Active Swap

Verified the active swap configuration using:

```bash
sudo swapon --show
```

The system displayed the active swap areas.

The traditional swap partition and the LVM-based swap were both successfully activated.

The LVM swap may appear through a device-mapper path such as:

```text
/dev/dm-4
```

rather than `/dev/vg200/swapVol`.

This is expected because LVM Logical Volumes are implemented through Linux's device-mapper subsystem.

Conceptually:

```text
/dev/vg200/swapVol
        ↓
   device-mapper
        ↓
    /dev/dm-X
```

---

## Final Storage Layout

The final configuration included both traditional and LVM-based swap:

```text
/dev/sde
├── /dev/sde1
│      ↓
│    swap
│      ↓
│    label: swappart
│
└── /dev/sde2
       ↓
      PV
       ↓
     vg200
       ↓
    swapVol
       ↓
      swap
```

The existing Volume Group was therefore composed of multiple Physical Volumes:

```text
/dev/sdc ───┐
            │
            ├── vg200
            │     └── swapVol
            │
/dev/sde2 ──┘
```

The exact Physical Volume allocation of the LV is determined by LVM's free Physical Extents within the Volume Group.

