## Lessons Learned

This lab demonstrated that swap does not have to be implemented as a traditional disk partition.

Swap can exist as:

```text
Partition → swap
```

or:

```text
LVM Logical Volume → swap
```

Both are valid Linux configurations.

The lab also reinforced the distinction between a Physical Volume and a Logical Volume.

A Physical Volume provides storage to a Volume Group:

```text
PV → VG
```

The Logical Volume is then allocated from the storage pool represented by the Volume Group:

```text
VG → LV
```

Therefore, when multiple PVs belong to the same VG, an LV may consume free Physical Extents from more than one PV.

This was demonstrated when the 180 MB `swapVol` could not fit entirely on the approximately 100 MB `/dev/sde2` PV.

---

## Real-World Application

Swap is commonly used on Linux servers to provide additional virtual memory capacity and to handle temporary memory pressure.

Administrators may configure swap as a partition or as an LVM Logical Volume.

LVM-based swap can be particularly flexible because the LV can be extended or managed as part of an existing LVM storage pool.

Persistent swap configuration through `/etc/fstab` ensures that the swap area is automatically activated after reboot.

Understanding `swapon`, `swapoff`, `mkswap`, `/etc/fstab`, LVM, and device-mapper is important when troubleshooting memory and storage configurations on Linux servers.

